<translate>
<!--T:1-->
Every feed is its own actor. Nobody declares or configures an account: the handle names the feed, and the account exists as long as the thing it follows exists. Because an account is a page or a category and never a person, there is nothing to impersonate.

<!--T:2-->
== Handles ==
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="text">
<kind>_<id>@<wiki host>
<kind>_<language>_<id>@<wiki host>
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:3-->
* <code>&lt;kind&gt;</code> is one or more lowercase ASCII words joined with <code>_</code>, taken from the [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Feed kinds</tvar>|list of kinds]].
* <code>&lt;id&gt;</code> is a decimal number. What it numbers depends on the kind; for the kinds in scope it is a page id.
* <code>&lt;language&gt;</code> names the language of a feed of [[<tvar name="2">Special:MyLanguage/Feed kinds#Translations</tvar>|translations]], which only <code>category</code> has: a language code the wiki knows, as MediaWiki writes it, such as <code>en</code> or <code>zh-hans</code>. A code starts with two or three letters, so no word of a kind is taken for one. A handle without it is the feed of the pages themselves.
* To parse a handle, strip the trailing <code>_&lt;digits&gt;</code> and match what is left against the list exactly. If nothing matches, strip a trailing <code>_&lt;language&gt;</code> too and match again. Anything that does not match, or whose target or language does not exist, is not an account.

<!--T:4-->
Examples: <code>category_4521@wiki.example.org</code>, <code>section_88@wiki.example.org</code>, <code>category_en_4521@wiki.example.org</code>.

<!--T:5-->
The title of the page is shown as the actor's <code>name</code>, so readers see <code>분류:개발 블로그</code>, not a number. The page can set a friendlier [[#Display name|display name]] instead.

<!--T:6-->
=== Why this shape ===
* Mastodon refuses a remote account whose username does not match <code>[a-z0-9_]+([.-]+[a-z0-9_]+)*</code> ([<tvar name="1">https://github.com/mastodon/mastodon/blob/1d67e7de9669a65c5f5fe2ee395341b1bbf9ab4c/app/models/account.rb#L73-L76</tvar> account.rb]). Page titles can hold Korean, spaces and colons, so a title cannot be a handle.
* Ids survive a page move. A title would not, and followers would be lost on every rename.
* <code>_</code> is an ordinary character everywhere, while <code>.</code> and <code>-</code> are separators that some software may treat differently.
* The kind comes first so that searching for <code>category</code> lists the category feeds together.
* The language sits between the kind and the id, so the kind still comes first and the handle still ends in the id. A code such as <code>zh-hans</code> keeps its <code>-</code>, which Mastodon's pattern allows between other characters.
* All feeds share one actor host, such as <code>fedi.example.org</code>, while handles keep the wiki's own host. Separate hosts per kind would work, but other servers treat each host as a separate instance. Keeping actors off the wiki host means wiki cookies are never sent with their requests.

<!--T:7-->
== Actor ==
{| class="wikitable"
! Property !! Value
|-
| <code>type</code> || <code>Service</code>
|-
| <code>id</code> || <code>https://&lt;actor host&gt;/actors/&lt;kind&gt;_&lt;id&gt;</code>, with the language between for a language's feed, built from the handle, never from the title
|-
| <code>preferredUsername</code> || The handle before the <code>@</code>
|-
| <code>name</code> || The page's [[#Display name|display name]] for the kind, or else its title with namespace. A language's feed adds the language's name in that language, as in <code>Dev blog (English)</code>
|-
| <code>summary</code> || One sentence saying what the feed publishes, in the wiki's content language, or for a language's feed in that language
|-
| <code>url</code> || The page on the wiki
|-
| <code>icon</code> || An <code>Image</code>: a raster thumbnail of the [[#Display name|icon]] the page set for the kind, or else for every kind. Without one, or when that file is missing or cannot be thumbnailed, an image of the wiki, the same for every feed, such as its touch icon or logo. Only a raster image such as PNG is used there, since Mastodon does not show SVG avatars. Left out when the wiki has none
|-
| <code>inbox</code>, <code>outbox</code>, <code>followers</code> || Under the actor id, such as <code>&lt;actor id&gt;/inbox</code>
|-
| <code>publicKey</code> || See [[#Keys|Keys]]
|}
A browser that opens the actor id is sent to the page with a 303. Mastodon keeps an actor's <code>url</code> only when it is on the host of the id, so its link to the profile is the id itself. The request counts as a browser's when its <code>Accept</code> names <code>text/html</code> and neither <code>application/activity+json</code> nor <code>application/ld+json</code>; any other request, including one with no <code>Accept</code> or only <code>*/*</code>, gets the JSON. Both answers carry <code>Vary: Accept</code>.

<!--T:10-->
== Display name ==
The followed page sets a display name with a magic word in its own wikitext:
* <code><nowiki>{{FEDIFEEDSNAME:</nowiki>&lt;name&gt;}}</code> applies to every feed of the page.
* <code><nowiki>{{FEDIFEEDSNAME:</nowiki>&lt;name&gt;|&lt;kind&gt;}}</code> applies to that kind only, and wins over the form without a kind. A category page that is also followed for its sections can name the two feeds apart this way.
* <code>section-unit=&lt;n&gt;</code>, where n is 1 to 6, sets the [[<tvar name="2">Special:MyLanguage/Feed kinds#section</tvar>|unit]] of the page's <code>section</code> feed: each heading with this many equals signs is one note. It can come before or after the kind.
* <code>icon=&lt;file&gt;</code> sets the actor's <code>icon</code> to a thumbnail of the file, 400 pixels wide, with or without the file namespace: <code><nowiki>{{FEDIFEEDSNAME:Dev blog|icon=File:Example.png}}</nowiki></code>. An SVG file works too, as its thumbnail is a PNG. It applies to the same feeds as the name, can come in any position, and is listed among the file's usage. A call that sets no name sets no icon either.
* The name is plain text: markup is dropped. An empty name, or a kind that is not in the [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Feed kinds</tvar>|list of kinds]], sets nothing. When the page sets the same one twice, the last wins.

<!--T:11-->
The display name changes only <code>name</code>. The handle, the id and the <code>summary</code> keep the page id and the title, so followers stay through a rename and the summary still says where the items come from.

<!--T:8-->
== WebFinger ==
<code>/.well-known/webfinger?resource=acct:&lt;handle&gt;</code> answers with a <code>self</code> link to the actor, on both the wiki host and the actor host. Mastodon fetches the actor and then asks the actor host's WebFinger for <code>preferredUsername@&lt;actor host&gt;</code>, which answers with the handle on the wiki host as its <code>subject</code>, so both directions must agree.

<!--T:12-->
== Instance actor ==
Besides the feeds, the wiki has one actor of its own, <code>wiki@&lt;wiki host&gt;</code>, as each Mastodon server has. It signs the wiki's fetches of remote documents, since a server in Mastodon's secure mode (<code>AUTHORIZED_FETCH</code>) refuses unsigned fetches of its accounts, and so of the keys that sign what they send. A feed's handle always ends in <code>_&lt;id&gt;</code>, so no feed can take the name <code>wiki</code>.
{| class="wikitable"
! Property !! Value
|-
| <code>type</code> || <code>Application</code>
|-
| <code>id</code> || <code>https://&lt;actor host&gt;/actors/wiki</code>
|-
| <code>preferredUsername</code> || <code>wiki</code>
|-
| <code>name</code> || The name of the wiki
|-
| <code>url</code> || The wiki's front page, such as <code>https://wiki.example.org/</code>
|-
| <code>icon</code> || The image of the wiki that feeds without an icon of their own show. Left out when the wiki has none
|-
| <code>inbox</code> || The shared inbox
|-
| <code>manuallyApprovesFollowers</code> || <code>true</code>
|-
| <code>publicKey</code> || See [[#Keys|Keys]]
|}
It has no outbox or followers. WebFinger answers for it as for a feed, with the wiki's front page as its profile page. A <code>Follow</code> of it, and anything else sent to it, is answered with 202 and dropped. A browser that opens its id is sent to the front page, as one that opens a feed's id is sent to the page.

<!--T:9-->
== Keys ==
The wiki has one key pair. Each actor publishes it under its own key id, <code>&lt;actor id&gt;#main-key</code>.
